 `audio_latency` over the debug protocol returns the probe report.
+
+## Input latency
+
+Input used to be applied once per frame by the emulation thread, and (in the
+threaded presenter) the main thread, which receives OS events, spent most of
+each frame blocked in the vsync present. Now:
+
+- the emulation loop applies input between scheduler rounds (threaded) or per
+  audio chunk (single thread), not just at the frame boundary;
+- `NDS_PRESENT_THREAD=1` moves the vsync'd present to its own thread so the
+  main thread only pumps input (~1 ms pickup, independent of present time).
+
+Measured with `tools/test_input_delay.py` (title repo), 80 jittered presses
+injected by an independent thread, pickup to applied-to-guest:
+
+| mode | mean | max |
+|---|---|---|
+| per-frame (old behaviour) | 8.5 ms | 16.7 ms |
+| single thread, per audio chunk | 0.7 ms | 2.4 ms |
+| threaded, main pumps between presents | 1.4 ms | 3.3 ms |
+| present thread | 0.5 ms | 2.0 ms |
+
+This is host pickup only. The guest still polls input about once per frame, so
+the game itself sees a press within one frame at worst.
+
+The adaptive audio floor now starts at 768 frames and decays 32 frames per 2 s
+once clean for 4 s: the cold-boot frames are slow and a low floor underruns
+there.
